# text = He saw that there was no mood of the mind that had not its counterpart in the sensuous life , and set himself to discover their true relations , wondering what there was in frankincense that made one mystical , and in ambergris that stirred one's passions , and in violets that woke the memory of dead romances , and in musk that troubled the brain , and in champak that stained the imagination ; and seeking often to elaborate a real psychology of perfumes , and to estimate the several influences of sweet-smelling roots and scented , pollen-laden flowers ; of aromatic balms and of dark and fragrant woods ; of spikenard , that sickens ; of hovenia , that makes men mad ; and of aloes , that are said to be able to expel melancholy from the soul .
